---- trace / diagonal / diag
ok   trace(3-D) default axis1=0, axis2=1: shape (4,), sum_i X[i,i,:]
ok   trace offset=+1: sum_i X[i, i+1, :]; offset=-1: sum_i X[i+1, i, :]
ok   trace(axis1=1, axis2=2): shape (2,), sum_j X[b, j, j]; axis1=-1, axis2=-2 same diagonal
ok   diagonal(3-D, offset=1, axis1=1, axis2=2): shape (2,3) with entries X[b, j, j+1]; offset=-2 -> X[b, j+2, j], shape (2,1)
ok   diagonal offset beyond the matrix returns an empty (M,0) diagonal; trace then 0
ok   diag(1-D, k=2) builds a (n+2)x(n+2) matrix with v on the 2nd superdiagonal; diag(2-D, k=-1) extracts the first subdiagonal
ok   trace of an int8 matrix is accumulated in the platform integer (documented sum dtype rule): [[100,0],[0,100]] -> 200, dtype int64
ok   trace(dtype=float) of int matrix returns float; trace of float32 stays float32
---- outer / kron / cross
ok   outer(a, b)[i,j] = a[i] b[j]; 2-D inputs are flattened first (documented)
ok   kron((2,3),(2,2)): shape (4,6), K[i*2+k, j*2+l] = a[i,j] b[k,l] (documented block structure)
ok   kron with different ndim: kron((3,), (2,2)) prepends 1s to the smaller (documented) -> shape (2,6) = kron(a.reshape(1,3), b)
ok   kron(scalar-like 1-D, 1-D) = outer(a, b).ravel(); kron(I2, B) is block-diagonal
ok   cross of 3-vectors = closed form; stacks (4,3)x(4,3) row-wise; anti-commutes; orthogonal to both
ok   cross of two 2-D vectors returns the scalar z-component (1*4 - 2*3 = -2); mixed 2-D/3-D treats the 2-D one as z = 0
ok   cross with 2-D vectors emits no warning on 1.x (deprecated only in 2.0)
ok   cross with 4-vectors raises ValueError (documented 'dimension ... does not equal 3')
ok   cross axisa=0, axisb=0, axisc=0 on (3,4) inputs: result (3,4) with column i = cross(a[:,i], b[:,i]); axis=0 equivalent; axisc=-1 puts the vectors in rows
ok   cross broadcasts a single vector against a stack
---- tensordot
ok   tensordot(a (3,4,5), b (4,5,2), axes=2): sums the last 2 axes of a with the first 2 of b -> (3,2)
ok   tensordot axes=1 (a (3,4,5), b (5,6)) -> (3,4,6) = a @ b; axes=0 -> outer product shape (3,4,5,5,6)
ok   tensordot axes=([1,0],[0,1]) (a (3,4,5), b (4,3,2)): sum_{i,j} a[i,j,k] b[j,i,l] -> (5,2)
ok   tensordot axes=(2, 0) as a pair of ints (a (3,4,5), b (5,6)) -> (3,4,6); axes=([2],[0]) same; axes=(0,1)? contracts a-axis 0 with b-axis 1 on (3,4,5)x(4,3,2) -> (4,5,4,2)
ok   tensordot with mismatched contraction lengths raises ValueError
---- einsum
ok   einsum 'ii' trace equals explicit loops  (shape ())
ok   einsum 'ii->i' diagonal equals explicit loops  (shape (3,))
ok   einsum 'ij->ji' transpose equals explicit loops  (shape (4, 3))
ok   einsum 'ij->' full sum equals explicit loops  (shape ())
ok   einsum 'ij->i' row sums / 'ij->j' column sums equals explicit loops  (shape (4,))
ok   einsum 'ij,jk->ik' matmul equals explicit loops  (shape (3, 5))
ok   einsum 'ij,jk' implicit output = 'ik' (alphabetical free indices) equals explicit loops  (shape (3, 5))
ok   einsum 'ij,jk->ki' transposed matmul equals explicit loops  (shape (5, 3))
ok   einsum 'bij,bjk->bik' batch matmul equals explicit loops  (shape (2, 3, 5))
ok   einsum '...ij,...jk->...ik' ellipsis batch matmul equals explicit loops  (shape (2, 3, 5))
ok   einsum '...ij,jk->...ik' ellipsis broadcasting a 2-D operand equals explicit loops  (shape (2, 3, 5))
ok   einsum 'i,j->ij' outer equals explicit loops  (shape (3, 4))
ok   einsum 'i,i->' inner / 'i,i' implicit equals explicit loops  (shape ())
ok   einsum 'ij,ij->ij' Hadamard equals explicit loops  (shape (3, 4))
ok   einsum 'ij,ji->' trace of product equals explicit loops  (shape ())
ok   einsum 'iij->ij' repeated index on one operand (partial diagonal) equals explicit loops  (shape (3, 4))
ok   einsum 'iij->j' partial diagonal then sum equals explicit loops  (shape (4,))
ok   einsum 'ijk->kji' 3-D permutation equals explicit loops  (shape (4, 3, 2))
ok   einsum 'ijk,k->ij' contraction with a vector equals explicit loops  (shape (2, 3))
ok   einsum 'i,i,i->' three operands equals explicit loops  (shape ())
ok   einsum 'ij,jk,kl->il' three-matrix chain equals explicit loops  (shape (3, 5))
ok   einsum 'ijk,jk->i' broadcasting-style contraction equals explicit loops  (shape (2,))
ok   einsum 'i...->...' sum over the first axis with ellipsis equals explicit loops  (shape (3, 4))
ok   einsum optimize=True / 'greedy' / 'optimal' give results identical to optimize=False on a three-matrix chain (exact ints)
ok   einsum four-index chain 'bij,bjk,kl->bil' with optimize=False/True/'optimal'/path from einsum_path all equal the explicit loops
ok   einsum_path returns (path, string_repr) with path = ['einsum_path', (pair), (pair)]: n_operands - 1 = 2 contraction tuples
   einsum_path greedy contraction order: [(0, 1), (0, 1)]
ok   einsum 0-d result with optimize=False is a scalar (documented); with out= it is the given array
ok   einsum 'i,i' on int8 [100,100,100]: result dtype int8 (input dtype kept), 30000 wraps to 48 (documented overflow semantics: no promotion, no error); np.dot the same; np.sum(i8*i8) would also be int8 -- np.sum(i8, dtype=int64) needed  (einsum 48, dot 48)
ok   einsum dtype=np.int64 on int8 operands accumulates in int64: exact 30000; dtype=float64 gives 30000.0
ok   einsum with out= of dtype float64 from int operands requires casting: casting='safe' (default) accepts int->float; result exact
ok   einsum invalid subscripts: mismatched dimension raises ValueError; output index not in inputs raises ValueError
ok   einsum on float32 operands keeps float32 and matches the mpmath product of the float32 values to 1e-5 relative
ok   einsum 'ij,jk->ik' float64 vs mpmath product (30x30) to 1e-13 relative
---- matmul / dot
ok   dot(a (2,3,4,2), b (2,3,2,5)): shape (2,3,4,2,3,5), dot(a,b)[i,j,k,m,n,p] = sum(a[i,j,k,:] * b[m,n,:,p]) (documented 'last axis of a and second-to-last of b')
ok   matmul(a (2,3,4,2), b (2,3,2,5)): shape (2,3,4,5), batch-wise product (documented 'stacks of matrices are broadcast together')
ok   matmul broadcasting of stacks: (2,1,4,2) @ (1,2,2,5) -> (2,2,4,5) with [i,j] = a[i,0] @ b[0,j]; a 2-D b broadcasts against a 4-D a
ok   dot with a 1-D b: sum product over the last axis of a (N-D) and b; equals matmul for that case
ok   matmul with 1-D operands: (4,) @ (4,5) -> (5,) (prepended 1 removed); (3,4) @ (4,) -> (3,); (4,) @ (4,) -> 0-d scalar
ok   matmul with a scalar raises ValueError (documented 'scalars not allowed'); dot with a scalar multiplies
ok   @ operator equals np.matmul for 2-D, stacked and 1-D cases
ok   matmul/dot of 2-D int matrices exact vs Python ints; matmul of int8 keeps int8 (wraps)
   float32 dot of 1e6 uniform(0,1) pairs: truth 250068.192232; np.dot(float32) = 250068.125000 (rel err 2.69e-07, dtype float32); np.dot(float64) rel err 4.07e-15; np.sum(a*b) float32 (pairwise) rel err 2.31e-07
ok   np.dot float32 on 1e6 elements: result float32 with relative error below 1e-4 (BLAS sdot, float32 accumulation; reported above); float64 dot within 1e-13
---- vdot / inner / vecdot / matvec / vecmat
ok   vdot(a, b) = sum conj(a) b (documented conjugation of the FIRST argument); dot(a, b) does NOT conjugate; vdot(b, a) = conj(vdot(a, b))
ok   vdot flattens multidimensional arguments (documented): vdot((2,2), (2,2)) = vdot of the raveled arrays
ok   inner(a (2,3,4), b (5,4)): shape (2,3,5), inner[i,j,k] = sum_l a[i,j,l] b[k,l] (sum over LAST axes of both, documented); inner with a scalar multiplies
ok   inner of complex 1-D does not conjugate (unlike vdot)
---- array-API aliases / transposes
ok   np.transpose of a (2,3,4) stack reverses ALL axes by default (documented) -> (4,3,2); axes=(0,2,1) transposes the matrices
ok   np.linalg.transpose (undocumented helper in every version) swaps the last two axes (source: 'swapaxes(a, -1, -2)'), unlike np.transpose
---- LinAlgError
ok   LinAlgError subclasses only Exception before 1.25 (source 'class LinAlgError(Exception)'): 'except ValueError' does NOT catch it
ok   LinAlgError raised by: solve singular, solve non-square, inv singular, inv 1-D, cholesky non-PD, qr 1-D, eig NaN, eigh 1-D, lstsq incompatible, tensorinv non-square, tensorsolve singular, matrix_power singular neg, det 1-D, cond empty, svd 1-D, matrix_power non-square, slogdet non-square
ok   NOT LinAlgError (by design): matrix_power(A, 2.5) TypeError; norm(v, 'bad') ValueError; solve with mismatched b ValueError; matmul shape mismatch ValueError; multi_dot([A]) ValueError
ok   singular-matrix LinAlgError message is 'Singular matrix'; non-PD cholesky message 'Matrix is not positive definite'  (LinAlgError('Singular matrix'); LinAlgError('Matrix is not positive definite'))
---- result dtype rules
ok   int64 input -> float64 result for inv, det, eigvalsh, cholesky, pinv, svd s, qr R, eig w, eigh V, solve, lstsq x, norm, slogdet logabsdet, cond, matrix_power -1
ok   float32 input stays float32 (documented 'float32 stays float32') for the same functions (SPD matrix, real eigenvalues)
ok   complex64 input -> complex64 results (real-valued outputs s, norms, eigvalsh, logabsdet, cond -> float32)
ok   mixed dtypes: solve(float32 A, int b) -> float64; solve(float32 A, complex64 b) -> complex64; solve(float64 A, complex64 b) -> complex128
ok   float16 and longdouble inputs raise TypeError in linalg (source _commonType: 'array type ... is unsupported in linalg')
ok   object-dtype input raises TypeError in inv/eig
ok   float32 results agree with the float64 truth to 1e-5 relative: inv, det, eigvalsh, cholesky, svd s (SPD 3x3)
